p \\partial A = \\emptyset$\n3. $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A$"] [17592186192337 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A$, denoted $\\partial A$, is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A$.\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4063/Source/Images/3933c33c218efa0a3c94b16611464247.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A$, denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, A$, is the set containing all the interior points of $A$. It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = A - \\partial A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4063/Source/Images/b920371607ced7ab80764cc5816233ad.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A - \\partial A = \\textrm{int}\\, A$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\textrm{int}\\, A \\cap \\partial A = \\emptyset$, where $\\emptyset$ denotes the empty set.\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186190916 17592186190917 17592186190918 17592186190919 17592186190920 17592186190922 17592186190923 17592186190924 17592186190925 17592186190926 17592186191084 17592186191085 17592186191086 17592186191087 17592186191088] "immutable_fields" [[17592186190915 :answer-field/correct] [17592186190915 :answer-field/id] [17592186190915 :answer-field/key] [17592186190915 :answer-field/type] [17592186190921 :answer-field/correct] [17592186190921 :answer-field/id] [17592186190921 :answer-field/key] [17592186190921 :answer-field/type] [17592186191083 :answer-field/correct] [17592186